{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfXVzDCp3cxTPTYqpeK2L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Both deliverables are written and the full 500-credit budget was spent.\n\n**Answers** in `/app/answers.md`:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Q1 (B pass rate, full verification) | 0.757 |\n| Q2 (B minus A) | +0.021 |\n| Q3 | SHIP |\n\n**How I got there.** A 40-item random pilot with verification, review and crowd labels for both models showed that the LLM judge and the expert reviewer both accept long B responses that full verification rejects. B's responses are 3.4 times longer than A's, so the judge's headline gap of +0.14 is mostly a length artifact. I then bought expert review for the whole pool as a cheap stratifier and spent the remaining budget on verification concentrated in B's long review-passed responses. In that stratum only 20 of 34 verified responses were actually correct, while every shorter review-passed response verified correct and review-failed responses were almost always wrong. A stratified estimator over those groups gives the numbers above. Finer bins and a logistic length model agree within 0.01.\n\n**Trust.** Q1 is well pinned, with a 95% interval of roughly 0.72 to 0.79. Q2 is the weak point. Its standard error is about 0.02, the same size as the estimate, so the SHIP call is a point estimate with roughly a 79% chance of being the right sign. The report at `/app/report.md` says this plainly and notes that verifying the remaining 93 long review-passed B responses would settle Q3 for about 186 credits."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2054,"cache_read_input_tokens":41509,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2054,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":507,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4561